factors, at the time of diagnosis, that determine the prognosis for remission of epilepsy. A prospective community‐based
cohort study of 792 patients recruited at the time of their first diagnosis of epileptic seizures was undertaken; in 
those classified 6 months after presentation, the median follow‐up period was 7.2 years (quartiles at 6.2 and 8.2 years)
after presentation. We analyzed data from 6 months after the first identified seizure, which prompted the dianosis of 
epilepsy, to allow us to factor in those aspects contingent on a diagnostic assessment. Baseline clinical and 
